Developer helpers. Nothing here is built into the twin, linked against it,
or needed to compile, test, or run the project.

  twin_sim      headless sim-runner. C source, built by the top-level
                CMake build into build/twin_sim(.exe). Logs each run to a
                SQLite database.
  plot_run.py   plots a run out of that database. Python 3, needs
                matplotlib (pip install -r tools/requirements.txt).


twin_sim

Steps the digital-twin core (engine + thermal + sync, plus the sensor
model) forward over a scripted throttle/altitude mission and logs a
time-series to a SQLite database -- one shared `runs` + `samples` schema
(src/data/run_log.h), auto-created on first use. Run parameters and errors
go to stderr; on completion it prints `run_id=N uuid=... db=PATH` to
stdout so a script knows which run to read back. Links engine_core only;
no SDL.

Build:

  cmake --build build --target twin_sim

Usage:

  twin_sim [--profile NAME] [--dt SECONDS] [--duration SECONDS]
           [--load NM] [--seed N] [--sensor] [--db PATH]
           [--cyl N:KEY=VAL ...] [--fault-at SECONDS]
           [--list] [--help]

  --profile NAME   mission profile (default: idle). -p also works.
  --dt SECONDS     integration step and sample spacing (default: 0.02)
  --duration SEC   total simulated time (default: the profile's own)
  --load NM        constant external load torque, N*m (default: 8)
  --seed N         RNG seed for the --sensor noise stream (default: 1)
  --sensor         add the 10 noisy sensor columns next to ground truth
  --db PATH        SQLite database to log into (default: runs/twin_sim.db;
                      its parent directory is created if missing)
  --cyl N:KEY=VAL  set one trim on cylinder N (1-based); repeatable. KEY:
                     inj    injector flow trim   (1.0 nominal)
                     comp   compression trim     (1.0 nominal)
                     spark  spark offset, deg    (0.0 nominal, + = retard)
                     leak   intake leak fraction (0.0 nominal, + = leaner)
                     cool   cooling trim         (1.0 nominal, <1 = hotter)
  --fault-at SEC   hold every --cyl trim until this time (default 0)
  --list           print the profile list and exit
  --help           print usage and exit

Profiles:

  idle             closed throttle, sea level
  takeoff          throttle ramp to WOT, ground roll then 10 m/s climb
  cruise-climb     0.75 throttle, 5 m/s climb capped at 4500 m
  hot-day          0.80 throttle, sea level, ISA +25 C
  rapid-throttle   0.20 <-> 1.00 square wave, 20 s period, sea level

Database schema:

One shared file (many runs, not one file per run) with two tables:

  runs      one row per run: id, uuid, source ("twin_sim"/"dashboard"),
            profile, dt, duration_s, load_nm, seed, engine_spec,
            with_sensor, started_at, ended_at, status, synced_at
  samples   one row per timestep: run_id, t, throttle, alt_m, ambient_c,
            airspeed_ms, cool_index, one REAL column per entry in
            model_channels() (src/model/channels.c) -- currently rpm,
            map_kpa, torque_nm, cht_c, egt_c, oil_c, cht_c_1..4, egt_c_1..4,
            air_gps, fuel_kgph, fuel_press_kpa, lambda_1..4, oil_press_kpa,
            bus_v, alt_a, alt_field_a, batt_soc -- plus s_rpm/s_rpm_ok/
            s_map_kpa/s_map_ok/s_cht_c/s_cht_ok/s_egt_c/s_egt_ok/s_oil_c/
            s_oil_ok, populated only when that run used --sensor (NULL
            otherwise, not 0)

`uuid` and `synced_at` exist for the (future) Go sync tool, which pushes
runs where `synced_at IS NULL` to a server and marks them.

New physics channels don't need this file, twin_sim.c, or plot_run.py
edited -- the samples schema and every column plot_run.py can plot are
both generated from model_channels() at runtime.

Poking at a run directly, e.g. with the sqlite3 CLI:

  sqlite3 runs/twin_sim.db "select id, profile, status from runs;"
  sqlite3 runs/twin_sim.db "select t, rpm, cht_c from samples where run_id=1;"

Examples:

  twin_sim --list
  twin_sim --profile cruise-climb
  twin_sim -p rapid-throttle --sensor --dt 0.01 --duration 90
  twin_sim -p takeoff --duration 120 --cyl 3:leak=0.35 --cyl 2:cool=0.82 \
           --fault-at 45 --db runs/fault.db


plot_run.py

Reads one run out of a twin_sim database and draws it as a plain stacked
multi-panel figure (default matplotlib styling) with a two-line metadata
header: speed/command, temperature, manifold/torque, and altitude panels.
Max markers are placed on CHT and EGT. Columns are matched by name, so
extra columns added later are ignored rather than shifting the layout.
--y overrides the layout with an explicit column list on a single panel.

Run metadata (profile, dt, duration, load, seed, sensor on/off, started_at)
comes straight from the run's row in the database -- always present,
nothing to parse.

Setup:

  pip install -r tools/requirements.txt

Usage:

  python tools/plot_run.py DB [options]
  python tools/plot_run.py [options] --sim TWIN_SIM_ARGS...

  DB                  path to a twin_sim SQLite database
  --run ID            run id to plot (default: the most recent in DB)
  --y COL [COL ...]   plot exactly these columns, skip the panel layout
  --sensor           overlay the s_* channels on their truth lines
  --limits           overlay caution / redline lines (off by default)
  --title TEXT       replace the metadata header with this text
  --out FILE         save the figure instead of opening a window
  --width  N         figure width in inches (default: 11)
  --height N         figure height in inches (default: 8)
  --twin-sim PATH    twin_sim binary for --sim (default: build/twin_sim)

--sim consumes every argument after it and hands them to twin_sim, then
reads back whichever run it just logged (twin_sim reports its run_id/db on
stdout on completion). Put plot_run options before --sim. A --sensor
inside the --sim args turns on the overlay here too.

The --limits caution/redline values are placeholders in LIMITS at the top
of plot_run.py -- tune them alongside the physics constants.

Examples:

  python tools/plot_run.py runs/twin_sim.db
  python tools/plot_run.py runs/twin_sim.db --run 3
  python tools/plot_run.py runs/twin_sim.db --y cht_c egt_c oil_c
  python tools/plot_run.py --out runs/rt.png --sim -p hot-day --sensor


Notes

Run databases belong in runs/ (gitignored). They're regenerable from
twin_sim, so don't commit them.
